  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS throughput, which immediately rules out `iterative` (1 result every N+3 cycles, so even at 200 MHz with N=10 it gives only ~18 MSPS... wait, 200MHz/13 = 15.4 MSPS, that actually meets it. But iterative has the longest latency and the shared datapath with barrel shifters typically has a longer critical path than pipelined variants, making it hard to hit high Fmax. More importantly, iterative produces one result every N+3 cycles, so to get 1 MSPS it needs Fmax >= (N+3) MHz. With N~12, that's ~15 MHz, which is trivially achievable. However, iterative's area is dominated by the barrel shifter datapath which is wide. Let me reconsider: iterative actually CAN meet throughput easily. But its area (LUTs+FFs) is typically higher than pipelined_m because the barrel shifter is a large combinational block. The key insight is that pipelined_m with m=4-8 has very few FFs (only N/m stages registered) and 1/cycle throughput, making it the strongest area candidate. I'll keep iterative out of round 1 to focus budget on the three families that clearly can meet constraints with competitive area. If pipelined_m and pipelined don't dominate, I can add iterative in round 2. Ranges are narrowed: data_width 12-18 because max_abs_err <= 2^-10 requires accuracy_bits >= 10; with n_iter=10-16 and modest guards, W=12-14 is typically sufficient, and W>18 wastes area. n_iter 10-16 balances accuracy vs area. angle_guard and frac_guard kept small (0-2) since the error budget is tight but not extreme. Rounding included as both options since it affects accuracy at the LSB level."
